"[2601.15394v1] Memorization Dynamics in Knowledge Distillation for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15394v1)\nciteturn27730view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15394v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Jan 2026 (this version), latest version 8 Aug 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Memorization Dynamics in Knowledge Distillation for Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Jaydeep Borkar , cite7†Karan Chadha , cite8†Niloofar Mireshghallah , cite9†Yuchen Zhang , cite10†Irina-Elena Veliche , cite11†Archi Mitra , cite12†David A. Smith , cite13†Zheng Xu , cite14†Diego Garcia-Olano L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Memorization Dynamics in Knowledge Distillation for Language Models, by Jaydeep Borkar and 8 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite15†View PDF cite16†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Knowledge Distillation (KD) is increasingly adopted to transfer capabilities from large language models to smaller ones, offering significant improvements in efficiency and utility while often surpassing standard fine-tuning. Beyond performance, KD is also explored as a privacy-preserving mechanism to mitigate the risk of training data leakage.\nL17: While training data memorization has been extensively studied in standard pre-training and fine-tuning settings, its dynamics in a knowledge distillation setup remain poorly understood. In this work, we study memorization across the KD pipeline using three large language model (LLM) families (Pythia, OLMo-2, Qwen-3) and three datasets (FineWeb, Wikitext, Nemotron-CC-v2).\nL18: We find: (1) distilled models memorize significantly less training data than standard fine-tuning (reducing memorization by more than 50%); (2) some examples are inherently easier to memorize and account for a large fraction of memorization during distillation (over ~95%); (3) student memorization is predictable prior to distillation using features based on zlib entropy, KL divergence, and perplexity; and (4) while soft and hard distillation have similar overall memorization rates, hard distillation poses a greater risk: it inherits $2.7\\times$ more teacher-specific examples than soft distillation.\nL19: Overall, we demonstrate that distillation can provide both improved generalization and reduced memorization risks compared to standard fine-tuning.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2601.15394 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2601.15394v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.15394†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jaydeep Borkar [cite20†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 21 Jan 2026 19:04:40 UTC (10,756 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Sat, 8 Aug 2026 01:31:14 UTC (10,840 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Memorization Dynamics in Knowledge Distillation for Language Models, by Jaydeep Borkar and 8 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite15†View PDF L37:   * cite16†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite21†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite22†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L46: \nL47: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2026-01 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite28†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite31†Semantic Schol