2601.15482v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Jan 2026]\nL9: # Title:Martingale Foresight Sampling: A Principled Approach to Inference-Time LLM Decoding\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Huayu Li , cite6†ZhengXiao He , cite7†Siyuan Tian , cite8†Jinghao Wen , cite9†Ao Li L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Martingale Foresight Sampling: A Principled Approach to Inference-Time LLM Decoding, by Huayu Li and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Standard autoregressive decoding in large language models (LLMs) is inherently short-sighted, often failing to find globally optimal reasoning paths due to its token-by-token generation process. While inference-time strategies like foresight sampling attempt to mitigate this by simulating future steps, they typically rely on ad-hoc heuristics for valuing paths and pruning the search space.\nL17: This paper introduces Martingale Foresight Sampling (MFS), a principled framework that reformulates LLM decoding as a problem of identifying an optimal stochastic process. By modeling the quality of a reasoning path as a stochastic process, we leverage Martingale theory to design a theoretically-grounded algorithm.\nL18: Our approach replaces heuristic mechanisms with principles from probability theory: step valuation is derived from the Doob Decomposition Theorem to measure a path's predictable advantage, path selection uses Optional Stopping Theory for principled pruning of suboptimal candidates, and an adaptive stopping rule based on the Martingale Convergence Theorem terminates exploration once a path's quality has provably converged.\nL19: Experiments on six reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that MFS surpasses state-of-the-art methods in accuracy while significantly improving computational efficiency. Code will be released at cite12†this https URL†github.com .\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2601.15482 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2601.15482v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.15482†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Huayu Li [cite16†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 21 Jan 2026 21:34:29 UTC (351 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Martingale Foresight Sampling: A Principled Approach to Inference-Time LLM Decoding, by Huayu Li and 4 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite10†View PDF L36:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite17†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite18†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L45: \nL46: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-01 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite24†cs L51: cite25†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite30†What is Connected Pa