]\nL9: # Title:MARS: Unleashing the Power of Speculative Decoding via Margin-Aware Verification\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Jingwei Song , cite7†Xinyu Wang , cite8†Hanbin Wang , cite9†Xiaoxuan Lei , cite10†Bill Shi , cite11†Shixin Han , cite12†Eric Yang , cite13†Xiao-Wen Chang , cite14†Lynn Ai L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled MARS: Unleashing the Power of Speculative Decoding via Margin-Aware Verification, by Jingwei Song and 8 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite15†View PDF cite16†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Speculative Decoding (SD) accelerates autoregressive large language model (LLM) inference by decoupling generation and verification. While recent methods improve draft quality by tightly coupling the drafter with the target model, the verification mechanism itself remains largely unchanged, relying on strict token-level rejection sampling. In practice, modern LLMs frequently operate in low-margin regimes where the target model exhibits weak preference among top candidates.\nL17: In such cases, rejecting plausible runner-up tokens yields negligible information gain while incurring substantial rollback cost, leading to a fundamental inefficiency in verification. We propose Margin-Aware Speculative Verification, a training-free and domain-agnostic verification strategy that adapts to the target model's local decisiveness.\nL18: Our method conditions verification on decision stability measured directly from the target logits and relaxes rejection only when strict verification provides minimal benefit. Importantly, the approach modifies only the verification rule and is fully compatible with existing target-coupled speculative decoding frameworks.\nL19: Extensive experiments across model scales ranging from 8B to 235B demonstrate that our method delivers consistent and significant inference speedups over state-of-the-art baselines while preserving generation quality across diverse benchmarks.\nL20: Comments:  | 11 pages, 5 figures\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2601.15498 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2601.15498v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.15498†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jingwei Song [cite20†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 21 Jan 2026 22:03:06 UTC (3,973 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v2] Sat, 11 Apr 2026 20:55:13 UTC (3,973 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled MARS: Unleashing the Power of Speculative Decoding via Margin-Aware Verification, by Jingwei Song and 8 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite15†View PDF L38:   * cite16†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite21†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite22†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.LG\nL45: \nL46: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L47: \nL48: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2026-01 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite28†cs L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite32†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite33†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Litmaps (cite34†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL86: \nL87: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL88: 