†Yangyang Zhong , cite7†Yanmei Gu , cite8†Zhengqing Zang , cite9†Xiaomeng Li , cite10†Yuqi Ding , cite11†Xibei Jia , cite12†Yuting Shen , cite13†Zhenzhong Lan , cite14†Liwang Zhu , cite15†Weiping Liu , cite16†Junlin Zhou , cite17†Haisheng Liu , cite18†Zhong Xin Yu , cite19†Pengxin Luo , cite20†Donglian Qi , cite21†Yunfeng Yan , cite22†Junbo Zhao L12: View a PDF of the paper titled Parallelism and Generation Order in Masked Diffusion Language Models: Limits Today, Potential Tomorrow, by Yangyang Zhong and 16 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite23†View PDF cite24†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:Masked Diffusion Language Models (MDLMs) promise parallel token generation and arbitrary-order decoding, yet it remains unclear to what extent current models truly realize these capabilities. We characterize MDLM behavior along two dimensions -- parallelism strength and generation order -- using Average Finalization Parallelism (AFP) and Kendall's tau. We evaluate eight mainstream MDLMs (up to 100B parameters) on 58 benchmarks spanning knowledge, reasoning, and programming.\nL16: The results show that MDLMs still lag behind comparably sized autoregressive models, mainly because parallel probabilistic modeling weakens inter-token dependencies. Meanwhile, MDLMs exhibit adaptive decoding behavior: their parallelism and generation order vary significantly with the task domain, the stage of reasoning, and whether the output is correct.\nL17: On tasks that require \"backward information\" (e.g., Sudoku), MDLMs adopt a solution order that tends to fill easier Sudoku blanks first, highlighting their advantages. Finally, we provide theoretical motivation and design insights supporting a Generate-then-Edit paradigm, which mitigates dependency loss while retaining the efficiency of parallel decoding.\nL18: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL19: Cite as:  | cite25†arXiv:2601.15593 [cs.CL]\nL20:    | (or cite26†arXiv:2601.15593v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL21:    | cite27†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.15593†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL22: ## Submission history\nL23: \nL24: From: Yangyang Zhong [cite28†view email ]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2601.15609v1] When Sharpening Becomes Collapse: Sampling Bias and Semantic Coupling in RL with Verifiable Rewards (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15609v1)\nciteturn27730view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15609v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2601.15390v1] FedUMM: A General Framework for Federated Learning with Unified Multimodal Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15390v1)\nciteturn27730view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15390v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\n"
